      {
        "pkcontributionId": 154590,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 21,
        "headingId": 1159,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-31T14:43:22"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1121,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 1160,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 154591,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 21,
        "headingId": 1160,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>FB51.2 Ischaemic fasciitis</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-31T14:43:22"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1122,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 1161,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 154592,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 21,
        "headingId": 1161,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Not recommended:</em> atypical decubital fibroplasia; pseudosarcomatous fibromatosis.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-31T14:43:22"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1123,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 1162,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 154593,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 21,
        "headingId": 1162,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-31T14:43:22"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1124,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 1163,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 154594,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 21,
        "headingId": 1163,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Ischaemic fasciitis usually arises around the limb girdles, sacral region, and greater trochanter; the chest wall and back may also be affected. Ischaemic fasciitis usually develops in the deep subcutis, but involvement of the deep dermis, skeletal muscle, and tendinous tissue can also occur {18724246}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-31T14:43:22"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1125,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 1164,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 154595,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 21,
        "headingId": 1164,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>This lesion occurs as a painless mass with a median size of 47&nbsp;mm. Ischaemic fasciitis may be associated with immobility or debilitation in some cases {18724246}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-31T14:43:22"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1126,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 1165,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 154596,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 21,
        "headingId": 1165,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Ischaemic fasciitis mainly affects the elderly, with a peak incidence between the seventh and ninth decades of life, although the age range is wide. Men are affected slightly more often than women {1530110; 8426859; 18724246}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-31T14:43:22"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1127,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 1166,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 154597,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 21,
        "headingId": 1166,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Ischaemia caused by constant pressure or trauma to a predisposed region may contribute to the pathogenesis in some patients {1530110; 8426859; 28477272}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-31T14:43:22"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1128,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 1167,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 154598,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 21,
        "headingId": 1167,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-31T14:43:22"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1135,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 1174,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 154599,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 21,
        "headingId": 1174,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Ischaemic fasciitis presents as a white fibrous to tan-yellow lesion with central necrosis or occasionally cystic change {18724246}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-31T14:43:22"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1129,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 1168,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 154600,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 21,
        "headingId": 1168,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The histological hallmark of ischaemic fasciitis is a distinct zonal appearance. The central part of the lesion is characterized by a hypocellular area of fibrinoid degeneration/necrosis with or without pseudocystic degeneration or infarcted fat. The central area is surrounded by a granulation tissue&ndash;like vascular proliferation mixed with fibroblasts and myofibroblasts, some polygonal with amphophilic cytoplasm and a ganglion cell&ndash;like appearance, similar to proliferative fasciitis. The fibroblastic/myofibroblastic cells vary in size and shape. Fibrosis/fibrohyalinosis or myxoid stromal change, hyalinosis of vessel walls, vessels with fibrin thrombi, an inflammatory infiltrate, and extravasated erythrocytes may be observed. Mitotic activity can be seen, mainly in the granulation tissue&ndash;like areas, but is inconspicuous overall {1530110; 8426859; 18724246}. By immunohistochemistry, variable expression of SMA, desmin, and CD34 may be observed {18724246}. There is frequent FOSB expression; however, no <em>FOSB</em> or <em>FOS</em> gene rearrangements are detected {37461273}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-31T14:43:22"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1130,
    "bookid": 87,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 1169,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 154601,
        "bookId": 87,
        "chapterId": 21,
        "headingId": 1169,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Insufficient data</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-31T14:43:22"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1131,
